import json
from pathlib import Path
HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
R = json.loads((HERE / "results.json").read_text())
p1 = lambda x: f"{100 * x:.1f}%"; ci = lambda c: f"[{100 * c[0]:.1f}, {100 * c[1]:.1f}]"
L = []; w = L.append
best = R["gate"]["chosen_method"]
T25, TW = R["tests"]["calendar_2025"], R["tests"]["winter_2025_26"]
def S(t, m, lv="90%"): return R["tests"][t]["methods"][m][lv]
w("# Prediction range around the Hybrid forecast (branch `feature/prediction-range`)\n")
w("**RETROSPECTIVE.** The point forecast is the unchanged Hybrid. The range is added around it; it does not change the point forecast's MAE. Nothing in the production model, default, dashboard or Lambda was modified. Protocol: `config.json` (written before running).\n")
w("## 1. Answer in short\n")
c25, cw = S("calendar_2025", best), S("winter_2025_26", best)
w(f"* **What \"85\" can mean, and what the data say** (Hybrid, chosen method {best}, nominal 90% range):\n")
w("| reading of \"85%\" | calendar 2025 (344 days) | winter 2025-26 (104 days) | meets 85%? |\n|---|---|---|---|")
w(f"| exact AQI category of a single value (existing point forecast) | {p1(T25['point_forecast']['hybrid_exact_category'])} | {p1(TW['point_forecast']['hybrid_exact_category'])} | no |")
w(f"| **range accuracy**: actual category inside the predicted category range | {p1(c25['range_accuracy'])} {ci(c25['cont_ci95_7d'])} | {p1(cw['range_accuracy'])} {ci(cw['cont_ci95_7d'])} | yes |")
w(f"| school decision GO/CAUTION/INDOOR from the point forecast | {p1(c25['decision_accuracy_point'])} {ci(c25['dec_hit_point_ci95_7d'])} | {p1(cw['decision_accuracy_point'])} {ci(cw['dec_hit_point_ci95_7d'])} | yes in both, but this needs no range |")
w(f"* **The declared gate (range accuracy ≥ 85% and fewer than 3 categories in the range, both test sets) is technically passed** ({p1(c25['range_accuracy'])} at {c25['mean_categories_in_range']:.2f} categories; {p1(cw['range_accuracy'])} at {cw['mean_categories_in_range']:.2f}). **But the gate is weak and I do not want to overstate it:** the naive range \"point category ± 1\" already gets {p1(c25['naive_point_pm1_category_accuracy'])} at {c25['naive_mean_categories']:.2f} categories in 2025. The selected method {best} is not narrower than that naive range (it over-covers: PM2.5 coverage {p1(c25['pm25_coverage'])} against a 90% target). So the selected range adds honesty about uncertainty, not extra accuracy.")
m1_25, m1_w = S("calendar_2025", "M1"), S("winter_2025_26", "M1")
m1_25b = S("calendar_2025", "M1", "80%")
w(f"* **A better-behaved choice is M1** (simple conformal on log residuals): PM2.5 coverage {p1(m1_25['pm25_coverage'])} / {p1(m1_w['pm25_coverage'])} (target 90%), range accuracy {p1(m1_25['range_accuracy'])} / {p1(m1_w['range_accuracy'])} at {m1_25['mean_categories_in_range']:.2f} / {m1_w['mean_categories_in_range']:.2f} categories, i.e. narrower than the naive ±1 range. At 80% nominal: range accuracy {p1(m1_25b['range_accuracy'])} at {m1_25b['mean_categories_in_range']:.2f} categories. {best} was chosen by the pre-declared rule (closest to nominal coverage on a 2024 split) and I kept that choice; M1 is reported alongside because it generalised better.")
w("* **Ranges are wide.** A 90% range spans 2–3 of the 6 AQI categories; at most about 10% of days get a single-category range. In winter the mean width is about 145 µg/m³ (M1) because uncertainty grows with the pollution level.")
w(f"* **Where the range is genuinely useful: the school decision.** The decision range (GO/CAUTION/INDOOR spanned by the range) contained the actual decision on {p1(c25['decision_range_contains_actual'])} of 2025 days. Adopting the *most cautious* decision in the range cuts under-warnings (the forecast says GO/CAUTION but the day is worse) from {p1(c25['under_warning_rate_point(GO/CAUTION when worse)'])} to {p1(c25['under_warning_rate_cautious'])}, at the cost of more over-warnings ({p1(c25['over_warning_rate_point'])} → {p1(c25['over_warning_rate_cautious'])}) and lower plain decision accuracy ({p1(c25['decision_accuracy_point'])} → {p1(c25['decision_accuracy_cautious_from_range_top'])}). That is a policy choice for you, not a free improvement.\n")
w("## 2. Method\n")
w("* Point forecast: Hybrid, production recipe, seed 42 (as in `experiments/backtest_2025`).\n* Ranges in log space around the point forecast. **M1** global conformal quantiles of log1p residuals; **M2** per predicted-level bucket; **M3** XGBoost quantile regression (5%/95%) with conformal correction (CQR).\n* **Calibration is strictly earlier and out of sample:** for 2025, residuals from 2024 (models trained to 2023-12-31; archived 24 h forecast weather; 347 days from 2024-01-20); for winter 2025-26, 2024 plus Jan–Oct 2025 residuals (617 days). Days without archived forecast weather are not used, and nothing is substituted. Calibration always ends before the test starts (checked in the tests).\n* The method was chosen on a split of the 2024 calibration period only, never on test results. Nominal level 90% was declared in advance; 80% and 95% are sensitivity checks for M1/M2.\n* Intervals use a moving-block bootstrap (7-day blocks; 14-day in `results.json`). Both test sets reuse days already examined in earlier experiments.\n")
for t, lab in (("calendar_2025", "Calendar 2025"), ("winter_2025_26", "Winter 2025-26")):
    w(f"## 3{'a' if t.startswith('cal') else 'b'}. {lab} ({R['tests'][t]['test_days']} days, calibration {R['tests'][t]['cal_days']} days ending {R['tests'][t]['calibration_last_day']})\n")
    w("| method / nominal | PM2.5 coverage | mean width µg/m³ | range accuracy (95% CI) | categories in range | single-category days | naive ±1: accuracy / categories | decision range contains actual | under-warning point → cautious | over-warning point → cautious |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|")
    for m, lv in R["tests"][t]["methods"].items():
        for l, s in lv.items():
            star = " ← selected" if m == best and l == "90%" else ""
            w(f"| {m} {l}{star} | {p1(s['pm25_coverage'])} | {s['mean_width_ugm3']:.0f} | {p1(s['range_accuracy'])} {ci(s['cont_ci95_7d'])} | {s['mean_categories_in_range']:.2f} | {p1(s['share_single_category_range'])} | {p1(s['naive_point_pm1_category_accuracy'])} / {s['naive_mean_categories']:.2f} | {p1(s['decision_range_contains_actual'])} | {p1(s['under_warning_rate_point(GO/CAUTION when worse)'])} → {p1(s['under_warning_rate_cautious'])} | {p1(s['over_warning_rate_point'])} → {p1(s['over_warning_rate_cautious'])} |")
    s = S(t, best)
    w(f"\nSelected method on sudden-change days (n={s['sudden_change_days']['n']}): range accuracy {p1(s['sudden_change_days']['range_accuracy'])}, {s['sudden_change_days']['mean_categories_in_range']:.2f} categories; stable days (n={s['stable_days']['n']}): {p1(s['stable_days']['range_accuracy'])}, {s['stable_days']['mean_categories_in_range']:.2f} categories.\n")
w("## 4. Limitations\n* Only 2024–25 residuals are available for calibration (forecast archive starts 2024-01-19), and 2024 had a 4-station network while 2025 used 9–10 stations, so calibration and test targets are not identically defined (same caveat as the 2025 backtest).\n* Retrospective; the test days were examined before. 104 winter days give wide intervals.\n* Ranges are not conditioned on season or weather regime beyond the predicted level. The most useful regime-specific ranges (stubble-burning season) would need crop-fire data, which the project does not have; that feature was deliberately not attempted and is deferred until you supply data.\n* Coverage is marginal (averaged over days), not guaranteed on every season or on sudden-change days.\n")
w("## 5. Recommendation\n")
w(f"1. The range is **sound and honest**, and by the gate you set (85% range accuracy) it passes. Treat it as an **uncertainty display and a safety option**, not an accuracy gain: the point-forecast accuracy numbers do not change.\n2. If you want it in the product, use **M1 at 90%** (or show the 80% range for a narrower band) and state plainly that ranges are wide and usually span 2–3 AQI categories. Show the cautious school decision only if you accept ~{p1(c25['over_warning_rate_cautious'])} over-warning days in return for under-warnings near zero.\n3. I have **not** changed the backend, dashboard or Lambda. Doing so is a production-facing change; tell me which method and level to build, and I will implement it on this branch with tests, leaving V1 as the default point forecast.\n")
(HERE / "report.md").write_text("\n".join(L)); print("ok")
